// expect-error: is passed to two `unique` parameters
// SPEC 5.1. `unique` on a parameter lowers to LLVM `noalias`, which makes it the
// one annotation whose falsehood is undefined behaviour rather than a missed
// optimisation -- so the reachable way of making it false has to be an error.
//
// The axiom's local half is discharged by the language: a second *owning*
// reference in the declaring scope is already `use of moved value`. Parameters
// borrow, though, so `bump(p, p)` is two perfectly legal borrows, and nothing
// else in sema notices that it hands one object to two parameters each asserting
// it is the only one.
//
// What this does not catch is aliasing through two different names, which is
// what `unique` being an axiom rather than a proof means. That is also why
// deleting the annotation is always safe (SPEC 11 item 7): it can only ever
// remove an assertion, never add one.
import std.io
struct Buf {
    a: Int,
    b: Int
}

fn bump(unique x: Buf, unique y: Buf) -> Int {
    x.a = x.a + 1
    y.a = y.a + 1
    return x.a + y.a
}

fn main() -> Int {
    let p = Buf { a: 1, b: 2 }
    println(bump(p, p))
    return 0
}
